# Website clone

Recreate the website's static structure and visual design first. Animations and advanced interactions come later.

## Reference viewport

Desktop screenshots are `1920 × 953` at `100%` zoom. That is the viewport, not the content width. Measure the real container from the original site. If a mobile screenshot is provided, use its exact viewport as the responsive reference.

## Selected DOM is the structure source

When a DOM section is selected in Cursor Browser, that selection is the primary structural reference. Use it for hierarchy, section boundaries, containers, wrappers, text, images, buttons, links, cards, nesting, and positioning.

The screenshot shows what the section should look like. The selected DOM shows how it is structured. Use both.

Inspect the selected element and its parents and children before implementing. Read computed styles when available: font size, weight, line height, letter spacing, color, background, width, height, max-width, min-height, padding, margin, gap, border, radius, display, position, flex, grid, alignment, object-fit, and overflow.

Reproduce only properties that affect the visual result. Do not guess values that can be measured.

## Source priority

- Structure: selected DOM, then screenshot
- Appearance: screenshot, then computed styles, then DOM

## Static layout only

Do not add GSAP, scroll or entrance animation, parallax, hover motion, or decorative transitions. Match the static page first.

## React structure

Split the page into components (`Navbar`, `Hero`, sections, `CTA`, `Footer`). Do not put the whole page in one component. Reuse a component when the same UI pattern repeats.

## Tailwind

Style with Tailwind. Reuse existing tokens for repeated container width, spacing, type size, radius, and color. Avoid one-off arbitrary values when a token already exists.

## Responsive

Match how the original site changes between desktop and mobile: nav, stacking, type size, spacing, padding, image size, alignment, visibility, button size, and content order.

## Section workflow

One section at a time: inspect, read the DOM, check computed styles, compare to the screenshot, build the component, then fix spacing, type, size, and position before the next section.

If a value cannot be determined, approximate it and keep it easy to adjust.
